.TH TUXDISPLAY 1 "October 2026" "TuxDisplay 0.4.22" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tuxdisplay \- use an iPad or Android tablet as a secondary Linux display
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tuxdisplay
.RI [ command ]
.PP
.B tuxdisplay
.B launch
.I application
.RI [ argument ...]
.PP
.B tuxdisplay
.B usb
.BR connect | disconnect | status
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B TuxDisplay
creates a compositor-owned virtual monitor or captures the primary physical
monitor in a GNOME Wayland session and streams it to OpenDisplay on iPadOS or
Android. Android can alternatively use the open-source AVNC client through a
loopback-only Tight/JPEG VNC server and device-scoped ADB reverse tunnel.
This direct USB mode requires no IP
address, Internet connection, Wi-Fi, cellular service, or tethering. Windows
can be moved between the physical display and tablet in Extend mode. Mirror
mode shows and controls the existing primary desktop.
.PP
An authenticated browser viewer is available on trusted private networks.
Xorg and unsupported Wayland compositors use an isolated X11/noVNC workspace;
that workspace is not an extension of the current desktop.
.PP
Version 0.4.0 must not be used on GNOME Wayland because its touch path could
abort Mutter. Versions 0.4.3 and 0.4.4 have connection regressions, 0.4.9 can
stall capture while rearranging displays, and 0.4.11 can restart-loop a virtual
monitor after capture failure. Use 0.4.22 or newer for all documented stability,
frame-pacing, and cursor-performance fixes.
.PP
The GTK manager and three-state indicator are one single-instance application.
The manager can check GitHub Releases and install a verified update after an
explicit user action and PolicyKit authentication. Display streaming itself
does not require Internet access.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gui
Open or present the single-instance GTK manager. This is the default when no
command is given.
.TP
.B start
Start the per-user service and create the selected display backend.
.TP
.B stop
Stop streaming and remove the virtual monitor, when present, or fallback workspace.
.TP
.B restart
Stop and recreate the display. OpenDisplay, AVNC, and browser viewers must reconnect.
.TP
.BI "configure --resolution " "preset"
Save a resolution preset. Add
.B --restart
to apply it immediately. The command also accepts
.BR --fps ,
.BR --display-mode ,
and
.BR --encoder .
.TP
.B status
Show service state, display mode, active encoder, OpenDisplay and AVNC
connection state, sender/receiver frame telemetry, browser fallback URL, and
access PIN.
.TP
.B url
Print the preferred browser-fallback URL. Direct OpenDisplay USB does not use
this URL.
.TP
.B password
Show the six-digit browser access PIN.
.TP
.B password --reset
Generate a new browser PIN and invalidate the previous value after restart.
.TP
.BI "launch " "application [argument ...]"
Start an application in the current GNOME desktop. In X11 fallback mode, start
it in the isolated workspace.
.TP
.B usb connect
Start the display if necessary and wait for OpenDisplay on a trusted,
cable-connected iPad or an ADB-authorized Android device, or for AVNC on an
ADB-authorized Android device. When no receiver is available, TuxDisplay may
offer the optional USB gadget fallback on supported device-capable hardware.
.TP
.B usb disconnect
Stop the display connection and tear down a TuxDisplay USB gadget if one was
created.
.TP
.B usb status
Report trusted-device, direct OpenDisplay, AVNC, USB-network, and gadget
capability state.
.TP
.B tray
Compatibility alias that starts the same application in background mode. Its
indicator is gray when stopped, amber while waiting for a receiver, and green
when a receiver is viewing.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the selected display mode, required programs, networking, trusted iPad,
Android ADB authorization, and USB gadget capability.
.SH DISPLAY MODES
.SS GNOME Wayland Extend
Mutter creates a real virtual output named
.BR Meta-0 .
PipeWire captures only that output. GStreamer supplies H.264 to OpenDisplay
and demand-driven JPEG frames to AVNC or the browser fallback. Changed frames
are encoded immediately;
while the desktop is unchanged, one liveness frame per second replaces continuous
duplicate-frame encoding.
.PP
TuxDisplay remembers the complete logical arrangement under stable physical
monitor identities because Mutter gives each new virtual output a different
identity. Rearranging displays in GNOME saves that layout and refreshes
capture on the same PipeWire node without recreating the monitor or USB
connection.
.SS GNOME Wayland Mirror
Mutter captures the current primary physical monitor without creating or
reconfiguring a virtual output. TuxDisplay preserves the source aspect ratio
inside the selected stream resolution and maps OpenDisplay touch through any
letterbox borders to the mirrored monitor.
.SS OpenDisplay USB
TuxDisplay connects to OpenDisplay port 9000 through usbmuxd on iPadOS or a
dynamically allocated ADB loopback forward on Android. Video and input use the
public OpenDisplay version 3 framing protocol over the USB cable. No network
interface or browser PIN is involved. Android requires USB debugging and an
approved computer fingerprint.
.SS AVNC Android USB
TuxDisplay serves Tight/JPEG VNC on the host loopback interface and creates an
.B adb reverse
mapping for each authorized Android device. Install AVNC from F-Droid, open a
new connection to 127.0.0.1 on the configured VNC port, leave the username and
password empty in GNOME Wayland mode, and enable view scaling. Pointer, click,
scroll, and keyboard input are forwarded to Mutter. This compatibility path is
open source and offline, but OpenDisplay H.264 normally provides better motion
and power efficiency. Tight/JPEG supports TuxDisplay widths up to 2048 pixels;
use 2048x1536 or a smaller preset with AVNC. The 2160x1620 preset remains
available through OpenDisplay and the browser path.
.SS Browser fallback
GNOME Wayland serves an authenticated MJPEG viewer over HTTP. Use it only on
a trusted private LAN or cable network.
.SS X11 compatibility
Xvfb, Openbox, tint2, x11vnc, websockify, and noVNC provide a separate desktop.
Applications from the current desktop cannot be dragged into it.
.SH RESOLUTION
The supported presets are 1024x768, 1280x800, 1366x768, 1536x1152,
1920x1080, 2048x1536, and 2160x1620. The default is 1920x1080.
.PP
Most traditional iPads are close to 4:3. Use 1536x1152 for balanced motion and
power use, or 2048x1536 and 2160x1620 for additional sharpness. Many Android
tablets are close to 16:10, for which 1280x800 is appropriate. Use 1024x768 for
the lowest CPU, GPU, and bandwidth use.
.PP
Changing resolution restarts the video service. In Extend mode it also replaces
the virtual monitor, so GNOME can move windows from the removed output back to
a physical monitor. TuxDisplay restores the saved relative placement when
Meta-0 returns. In Mirror mode the physical monitor remains active and only the
encoded stream resolution changes. Reopen OpenDisplay if it does not reconnect.
.SH INPUT
OpenDisplay touch and Apple Pencil events are translated into a guarded
single-pointer stream. Tap, drag, pointer movement, and scrolling are
supported. Native multi-touch, Pencil pressure, and tilt are not forwarded.
.PP
The AVNC and browser paths also accept pointer, clicking, scrolling, and
keyboard events. AVNC gestures are translated by the client into standard VNC
pointer events; native Android multi-touch is not forwarded.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/config
accepts:
.PP
.nf
DISPLAY_MODE=extend
RESOLUTION=1920x1080
FPS=30
ENCODER=auto
CURSOR_MODE=embedded
KEEP_AWAKE_WITH_LID_CLOSED=0
DISPLAY_NUMBER=48
WEB_PORT=6080
VNC_PORT=5900
.fi
.TP
.B DISPLAY_MODE
accepts
.B extend
or
.BR mirror .
Mirror captures the current primary physical monitor and maps tablet input to
it; Extend creates the separate Meta-0 output.
.TP
.B RESOLUTION
sets the virtual-monitor mode in Extend mode and the encoded raster in Mirror
mode.
.TP
.B FPS
accepts 15, 30, or 60 and controls the virtual-monitor and advertised receiver
ceiling. The default is 30. Version 0.4.17 encodes damage-driven frames up to this
limit and uses only a one-frame-per-second liveness pulse while content is static.
Use 60 only when capture, sender, and receiver telemetry show that the host and
tablet sustain it.
.TP
.B ENCODER
accepts auto or software. Auto prefers VA-API H.264 acceleration and falls back
to the tuned x264 path when the required elements or driver are unavailable.
.TP
.B CURSOR_MODE
accepts embedded or overlay. Embedded is the compatible default and keeps the
pointer in captured video. Overlay avoids full-frame work for pointer-only
motion but requires a receiver that renders OpenDisplay cursor controls.
.TP
.B KEEP_AWAKE_WITH_LID_CLOSED
accepts 0 or 1. When enabled, TuxDisplay blocks system sleep and lid-switch
handling while the display service runs. This also blocks manual and idle
suspend. Stopping TuxDisplay releases the inhibitor.
.TP
.B DISPLAY_NUMBER
applies only to the X11 fallback.
.TP
.B VNC_PORT
is the Android-local AVNC port and the host loopback VNC port. GNOME Wayland
uses the embedded Tight/JPEG server; X11 fallback uses x11vnc with the displayed
PIN as its VNC password.
.TP
.B WEB_PORT
and the PIN apply only to browser access. Restart the service after editing.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TUXDISPLAY_FORCE_X11=1
Force the isolated X11/noVNC backend instead of GNOME Wayland mode.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/config
Display mode, resolution, frame-rate, lid behavior, and port settings, created
with private permissions.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/password
Generated browser PIN.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tuxdisplay/*.rfb
Authentication material for the VNC fallback when used.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tuxdisplay/
Connection state and application logs.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.nf
tuxdisplay status
tuxdisplay usb status
tuxdisplay doctor
systemctl --user status tuxdisplay.service
journalctl --user -u tuxdisplay.service --since "10 minutes ago"
.fi
.PP
In a healthy direct session, sender source and sent rates settle near the
configured rate, the pending queue remains small, and drops and recoveries stay
at zero. OpenDisplay receiver counters are non-normative; compare them with the
sender rates and visible motion before diagnosing a transport failure. Redact
the PIN, device labels, local addresses, and personal paths before publishing
diagnostics.
.PP
After a receiver has begun reporting health, TuxDisplay treats eight seconds
without another report during active video as stale. It marks the saved
telemetry stale, primes the receiver with the cached keyframe, and requests a
fresh IDR. If reports remain absent for fifteen seconds, it rebuilds the cable
session. Receivers that never advertise health telemetry are not subject to
this watchdog.
.PP
Version 0.4.17 also watches the host encoded-frame callback. If it stops for
eight seconds, TuxDisplay performs a bounded service recycle. A hardware
pipeline stall enables the software encoder for 24 hours. No more than two
automatic pipeline recoveries are allowed in ten minutes; further recovery is
suppressed and shown in pipeline diagnostics instead of creating a restart loop.
.PP
Version 0.4.20 defaults to embedding the cursor in captured video so it remains
visible on every receiver. The manager can instead select the low-power
OpenDisplay overlay introduced in 0.4.19. That mode tracks the XWayland pointer
at up to 60 Hz and sends deduplicated cursor control messages, so pointer-only
motion does not wake full-frame H.264 encoding, but the receiver must implement
the optional cursor and cursorImg messages. The status command reports the
selected cursor transport and its update count.
.SH SECURITY
OpenDisplay traffic uses the operating system's trusted-device usbmuxd channel
or a loopback-only ADB forward, but OpenDisplay protocol version 3 adds no
encryption or authentication. Trust only computers and tablets you control.
.PP
AVNC traffic uses an ADB reverse tunnel to a loopback-only host socket. The
GNOME Wayland compatibility server does not offer VNC authentication, so USB
debugging authorization is the trust boundary and other local processes can
reach the loopback port. Do not forward that port to a LAN or the Internet.
.PP
The browser service listens on local interfaces and uses PIN authentication
over ordinary HTTP. Never expose port 6080 to the public Internet or an
untrusted network.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Native desktop extension and primary-screen mirroring require GNOME Wayland.
Direct OpenDisplay transport is currently USB-only. Hardware acceleration
currently targets VA-API and otherwise falls back to software x264.
Receiver dimensions are not used to select or dynamically resize the stream.
Only one direct OpenDisplay receiver is supported at a time. AVNC uses JPEG
rather than the hardware H.264 path and may consume more CPU or deliver fewer
frames at high resolutions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tuxdisplay-usb (8),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
